 the pandemic she found herself  overwhelmed with juggling household  chores caring for her sons and working  on her non-profit remotely i was doing  the homeschooling while i was running my  organization in between i was doing the  laundry you know i mean doing the dishes  right ordering whatever i needed on  amazon  and so by the end of the day i was just  done you know i got covered and it  barely registered you know my liver  failed i had acne on my face as if i was  like 16 years old and it wasn't just me  in response to the pandemic reshma is  proposing a marshall plan to better  support working moms including wage  equity paid family leave and work  retraining programs when women return  from maternity leave you know moms are  not america's social safety net we're  one of the few countries that doesn't  offer a paternity allowance that's come  today in the form of a child tax credit  today the expanded child tax credit is  here but that was a revolutionary idea  that's been met with a tremendous amount  of resistance  as a mother a single mother like you  need to be there  for everything for your child i was  working at 3-1-1 i got a call  it was like an emergency call  and my supervisor like you can't use  your phone so if people need you they  have to call like the office and  you you would get connected through your  supervisor my child ended up at the  hospital and i'm in the middle of the  floor i heard das answering the phone  i'm instantly devastated but i'm also  embarrassed  frustrated  aggravated that i had to get this phone  call like this in front of this woman  standing here in the midst of everybody  i ended up losing the job because  um when you're out you have to call in  and tell them kind of what's going on or  the reason for your being out  and at that time i couldn't even talk  without crying that really  kind of threw me off as a person burnout  isn't just something that happens at  work it's something that happens when  you're you know a domestic child care  provider and so if you're trying to do  both of those things people's mental  health suffers this stress is compounded  during the pandemic with one piece of  research stating that working women have  reported increased levels of depression  by 83  compared to 36 percent of working men  crucially this can impact a woman's  ability to progress in her career and  when women are underrepresented at work  companies are less incentivized to  provide them the support they need women  do a tremendous amount of unpaid labor  almost 86 of the household chores and  the child care rearing is done by women  and so you simply can't raise your hand  for that promotion you can't start that  business you can't take on that nursing  degree that you've always dreamed about  because you have so much happening at  home and this is why we have enormous  levels of poverty when it comes to women  of color because they're in low-paying  jobs and they're not getting the support  that they need you know to thrive so i  lost my job stopped paying rent we went  to  live in the shelter one of the things  that i did with a lot of my free time  was volunteer at church we wanted to  show the scriptures like so if people  didn't have their bible they could just  look up on the screen i figured out what  device to use and what program to run it  i had some help but a lot of it is me  running it and doing it and implementing  it  one of my friends she saw that i had a  good hold to technology  on her friend's recommendation tanya  soon applies and gets accepted into a  coding boot camp it's a program where  you don't pay upfront  but it's also like an unpaid thing so  there was no income coming from there so  i'm in pursuit struggling  things are moving very very quickly when  it comes to the coronavirus i'm stating  the obvious to everyone here  overheads  i had to shift my whole life to make  sure that i supported and covered my  children i have to leave early every day  from 