een companies like linkedin and  other tech companies kind of offering  alternative child care last summer for  example some companies offered summer  camps and then in the public sector  we've seen biden making a big push for  what we call soft infrastructure which  would be universal child care for  children's ages three and four as well  as child care subsidies for those from  zero to five the current child tax  credit that was passed during the  pandemic offers families up to 300 in  monthly payments per child as part of  her marshall plan for mom's proposal  reshma is calling for these credits to  be extended indefinitely among other  benefits making the child tax credit  permanent getting affordable child care  you know passing paid leave once for all  the second piece is really working you  know on the private sector and making  sure that we build a work environment  that works for parents offering parental  leave offering flexibility finally  getting equal pay for equal work  with so many things going on we have to  choose what our children over our jobs  like we're capable and if we're paid  enough for the value that we do bring we  can actually be able to do a bit more  even as congress makes moves towards  financial assistance for working mothers  there are also many other issues  preventing women from fully returning to  work in person  from sexual discrimination to harassment  to microaggression at the workplace  and then it's the men who have face time  with their bosses and are getting the  promotions and are being noticed if  employers really want to create a  greater culture of belonging it does  require addressing uh systems and and  that takes money covet has given us this  opportunity to look at our society and  say you know what i want to build it  back better right like this is broken  and it is okay to acknowledge it it's  not just for moms but it's for dads it's  for millennials who don't want to have  kids it's for everybody to be like  we a